WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:08.120
These spiney spheres are plant pollen, as
seen under a microscope.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.360
They’re the culprit behind most seasonal
allergies.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.820
Pollen can cause your immune system to kick
into overdrive because your body sees the

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.920
pollen as a harmful intruder.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.360
As a result, the body produces histamines.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.680
Normally, they promote healing by increasing
blood flow and inflammation.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:30.659
But they can lead to the nasty symptoms we
associate with allergies — like sneezing,

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.980
runny noses, and watery eyes.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.060
Generally, people who suffer from allergies
have it bad for several weeks during certain

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.740
plants’ growing seasons.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:45.690
But because of climate change, the level of
pollen in the air is getting much worse.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:49.600
And that’s bad news — whether you suffer
from allergies or not.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:00.359
In order to grow and produce pollen, plants
need to generate energy through photosynthesis.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:06.120
That requires water, sunlight, and a third necessary ingredient, carbon dioxide.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:13.560
Carbon dioxide exists naturally in the atmosphere,
but as we burn fossil fuels like coal in power

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:18.980
plants or gasoline in our cars, we’re putting
more and more CO2 into the air.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:24.180
The more carbon dioxide there is in the air,
the more plants grow and produce pollen.

00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:26.070
Take a look at this graph.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:31.820
These bars are the level of carbon dioxide
in the atmosphere between 1996 and 2015.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:37.960
And this line is the amount of pollen produced
per ragweed plant, a common source of allergens.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:42.220
As carbon dioxide increased, the ragweed produced
more and more pollen.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:47.180
And pollen is an important part of a plant’s
reproductive cycle.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:53.180
So more pollen means more seeds, which means
more plants producing more pollen the following season.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.660
But CO2 is increasing the level of pollen
in another way as well.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.300
Carbon dioxide is a greenhouse gas.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:06.509
And it’s called that because it traps the
sun’s heat energy in the Earth’s atmosphere

00:02:06.509 --> 00:02:10.210
and is helping warm the planet — sort of
like a greenhouse.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:14.560
As the climate warms, there are more days
above freezing, which means more time that

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.640
plants are active and producing pollen.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:22.720
This graph shows the number of days with above-freezing temperatures in the US over the past 120 years.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:28.210
In the past 50 years, the US growing season
has increased by over 10 days, on average.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.900
And this isn’t happening at the same rate everywhere.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:37.800
This map shows the increase in pollen season
for ragweed between 1995 and 2015.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:42.910
While some places, like Texas, didn’t see
much change in their ragweed pollen season,

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:48.080
most places — especially those in the northern
US and southern Canada — saw pollen season

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.180
increase by almost a month.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.460
And this isn’t just happening in North America.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:59.620
Overall, most places around the world are
seeing longer growing seasons — and, as

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:02.040
a result, more days with pollen as well.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:10.140
Pollen levels and allergies are only going
to get worse from here if carbon emissions

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.120
continue the way they are.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.650
This is the current production level of grass
pollen, another common allergen source.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:20.150
By 2060, that level is expected to double.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.420
And by 2085, that amount will have more than
tripled.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:29.900
While allergies can be annoying for the 1
in 5 Americans that suffers from them, they

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:33.290
can usually be kept at bay with over-the-counter drugs.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:38.010
But in some, allergic reactions can lead to
severe reactions like anaphylaxis, where blood

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:40.750
pressure drops and airways start swelling
shut.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:46.060
And pollen grains can wreak havoc on people’s
lungs, even if they don’t suffer from seasonal allergies.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:51.060
In the US, Asthma attacks induced by pollen
have lead to more than 20,000 emergency room

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:52.620
visits per year.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:57.700
So if for no other reason than to protect
your sinuses, it’s important to reduce the

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:59.870
amount of CO2 we’re adding to the atmosphere.

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:05.360
It can help curb how much we’re warming
the planet and it can help slow the pollen-ocalypse.

